---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Passage = `THE SMART WATER METER

A city has replaced its water meters. The old ones were read by
hand once every three months. The new ones measure the flow
through the pipe every fifteen minutes and send the reading over
the mobile phone network to the utility.

Each reading arrives with the meter's identifier, the time it was
taken, and the strength of the network signal. The utility keeps
this alongside the reading itself.

The finer measurements let the utility spot leaks. A household
that uses water steadily all night almost certainly has a running
tap or a broken pipe, and the utility now writes to those
households within a week. Under the old system a leak could run
for three months before anyone noticed.

The utility also publishes neighborhood totals, with individual
households removed, for researchers. One published study found
that neighborhoods with higher water use also had higher rates of
household debt. A newspaper reported this as evidence that water
bills were pushing families into debt.

Some meters sit in basements where the mobile signal is weak.
Those meters report less often, and sometimes send readings that
are obviously wrong -- a flow rate no household pipe could carry.
The utility discards any reading above a threshold it has set.

The city offers a lower rate to households that agree to let the
utility control their water heater during peak demand. Taking part
requires a smartphone app.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Question Set 13: Reading Passage</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">
          Five questions &middot; one passage &middot; 12 minutes
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Is</h2>

          <p>
            <b>The second reading-passage set in the course.</b> Five of the
            seventy questions on the AP exam share one passage about a computing
            innovation, and reasoning about computing innovations is{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">28&ndash;33% of the multiple-choice section</b>{" "}
            &mdash; more than any other skill, including writing code.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>This one is harder than the first.</b> The Part 0 passage asked you
            to notice effects. This one asks you to notice effects <i>and</i> to
            apply what you have learned about data in this block &mdash; sampling,
            metadata, cleaning, and correlation are all in the passage, none of
            them named.
          </p>

          <h4>How to Do This One</h4>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <b>Read the whole passage first.</b> All of it, before you look at a
              single question.
            </li>
            <li><b>Set a timer for 12 minutes.</b> The reading is part of it.</li>
            <li>
              <b>Every answer is in the passage or follows from it.</b> Nothing
              here needs outside knowledge about water utilities. If you find
              yourself using something you already believed, check that the passage
              actually says it.
            </li>
            <li>Mark each answer <b>sure</b> or <b>unsure</b>, as always.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="passage">
          <h2>The Passage</h2>

          <Code
            code={Passage}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Read this before looking at the questions"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="questions">
          <h2>The Questions</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 1</h3>

          <p>
            The new meters take a reading every fifteen minutes instead of once
            every three months. This change is best described as:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) Increasing the sample rate</li>
            <li>(B) Compressing the data</li>
            <li>(C) Cleaning the data</li>
            <li>(D) Removing metadata</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 2</h3>

          <p>
            Each reading arrives with the meter's identifier, the time, and the
            signal strength. Which statement about that additional information is
            best supported by the passage?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) It is metadata, and deleting it would change the recorded flow values</li>
            <li>(B) It is metadata, and it is what lets the utility tell one meter's readings from another's</li>
            <li>(C) It is the primary data, and the flow reading is metadata about it</li>
            <li>(D) It is a compressed form of the flow reading</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 3 &mdash; Select all that apply</h3>

          <p>
            Which of these are supported by the passage as <b>problems with the
            data</b> the utility collects?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) Meters in weak-signal locations report less often than others</li>
            <li>(B) Some readings are impossible values that have to be discarded</li>
            <li>(C) The utility has set a threshold, so what counts as impossible is a decision it made</li>
            <li>(D) The readings are stored without any time attached</li>
            <li>(E) Neighborhood totals cannot be calculated from individual readings</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 4</h3>

          <p>
            The newspaper reported the study as evidence that water bills were
            pushing families into debt. Which is the strongest objection to that
            reporting?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) The study used neighborhood totals, so the water data is unreliable</li>
            <li>(B) Household debt cannot be measured accurately</li>
            <li>(C) The utility should not have published the data at all</li>
            <li>(D) Two things rising together does not establish that one causes the other, and something else could be raising both</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 5</h3>

          <p>
            The lower rate requires a smartphone app. Which effect does this most
            directly create?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) The utility collects less metadata from those households</li>
            <li>(B) Leak detection becomes less accurate across the city</li>
            <li>(C) Households without a smartphone cannot access the cheaper rate</li>
            <li>(D) The sample rate falls for participating households</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Five answers, each marked <b>sure</b> or <b>unsure</b>, plus how far
            you got when the timer went off.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>And one written answer, in a sentence or two:</b> the passage says
            the utility discards any reading above a threshold it has set. Name one
            way that could go wrong, using what you know from Activity 2.19.
          </p>

          <p>It is collected as <b>Homework Drop 10</b>, together with Debug Challenge: Data. <b>The drop is worth 5 points</b> for everything in it, graded on completion.</p>
        </section>

        <h1 class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash">
          Start the Timer
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
